<p>
Speech synthesis has come a long way since I first tried out <a href="https://emacspeak.sourceforge.net/">Emacspeak</a> in 2002. <a href="https://github.com/nazdridoy/kokoro-tts">Kokoro TTS</a> and <a href="https://github.com/OHF-Voice/piper1-gpl">Piper</a> offer more natural-sounding voices now, although the initial delay in loading the models and generating speech mean that they aren't quite ready to completely replace <a href="https://espeak.sourceforge.net/">espeak</a>, which is faster but more robotic. I've been using the <a href="https://github.com/remsky/Kokoro-FastAPI">Kokoro FastAPI</a> through my own functions for working with various speech systems. I wanted to see if I could get Kokoro and other OpenAI-compatible text-to-speech services to work with either <a href="https://github.com/brailcom/speechd-el">speechd-el</a> or Emacspeak just in case I could take advantage of the rich functionality either provides for speech-synthesized Emacs use. speechd-el is easier to layer on top of an existing Emacs if you only want occasional speech, while emacspeak voice-enables many packages to an extent beyond speaking simply what's on the screen.
</p>

<p>
Speech synthesis is particularly helpful when I'm
learning French because I can use it as a
reference for what a paragraph or sentence should sound
like. It's not perfect. Sometimes it uses liaisons
that my tutor and Google Translate don't use. But
it's a decent enough starting point. I also used
it before to read out IRC mentions and compile
notifications so that I could hear them even if I
was paying attention to a different activity.
</p>

<p>
Here's a demonstration of speechd reading out the following lines using the code I've just uploaded to <a href="https://codeberg.org/sachac/speechd-ai">https://codeberg.org/sachac/speechd-ai</a>:
</p>

